my/question-capture/13956996/assets/1baa0bec9c8cfdb2ce80d47e66f6ba54d34266267df3ee323a658c0f98a52f83.png)\n\nGiven that the points $X$ and $Y$ with coordinates $(1,4)$ and $(-1,-4),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{XY}:$\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(4-(-4))}^{2}+{(1-(-1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{68} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 17} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{17}}{2}=\\sqrt{17}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f93da5e0c7c35b25042ddb1ddc4ec09bd2ca0009b53563fe763380fdee0d4cbf"} {"question" "q-14792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=17"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2\\sqrt{17}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=34"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=68"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{17}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=17"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/assets/883828970a6deca7d7efdc201da679324f17ec916ba0b44b76f5af2718ead2b9.png)\n\nGiven that the points $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(3,5)$ and $(-3,-5),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{{(5-(-5))}^{2}+{(3-(-3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 34} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{AB}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{34}}{2}=\\sqrt{34}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{34})^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f93da5e0c7c35b25042ddb1ddc4ec09bd2ca0009b53563fe763380fdee0d4cbf"} {"question" "q-14793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=136"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2\\sqrt{34}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=34"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=68"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{34}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14793" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=34"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/assets/1023ca76971805ffc07c0397565723973a6a65359cade8d0bfbfa5a5b63f8a5c.png)\n\nGiven that the points $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(6,5)$ and $(-6,-5),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{AB}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{{(5-(-5))}^{2}+{(6-(-6))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{244} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 61} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{61}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{AB}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{61}}{2}=\\sqrt{61}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{61})^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =61.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/state.json" "file_sha256" "126d5c60449edccddcf6bf13cc0c9bd0a3e4a257a5d2fb553f71a7a57ddbad6f"} {"question" "q-14911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13956996/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f93da5e0c7c35b25042ddb1ddc4ec09bd2ca0009b53563fe763380fdee0d4cbf"} {"question" "q-14911" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :a